\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Benchmark scores drive model selection and research priorities for large language models, yet training data contamination is pervasive: trillion-token web crawls routinely ingest benchmark items \citep{oren2024proving}, and practitioners cannot determine how much of a reported score reflects genuine capability versus memorization \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu,chang2025benchmarking}. Despite twenty mitigation strategies \citep{sun2025emperor}, contamination-free benchmarks \citep{white2024livebench}, and dynamic regeneration \citep{dong_2024_cdd}, a fundamental question remains: what is the actual effect of contamination on aggregate scores? The implicit assumption is uniform inflation, but this has never been tested across model families.

We perform controlled SFT injection (LoRA, five dosage levels) into five models spanning four independent families from four labs (Alibaba, Microsoft, Allen AI, Google) and discover five distinct dose-response patterns---from immunity (Phi-4 14.7B, $\pm$0.5pp on MMLU) to catastrophic U-type collapse (Qwen-14B, $-$10pp). A baseline ability threshold at ${\sim}$64\% MMLU separates models that benefit from contamination (OLMo-2 $+$3.2pp, Gemma-2 $+$3.8pp) from those harmed or immune. Counter-intuitively, the 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B. Using ContamCal, a two-stage pipeline combining multi-signal item filtering with contamination-augmented IRT (2PL+$\gamma_k$), we provide the statistical machinery for this characterization.

Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Five-pattern contamination taxonomy.} Controlled injection across four families reveals five dose-response patterns governed by a ${\sim}$64\% baseline threshold, directionally consistent across MMLU and ARC-Challenge. Within-family domain analysis reveals additional heterogeneity (Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$).
    \item \textbf{Signal failure characterization.} Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits directional reversal at 0.5B (Cohen's $d = -1.55$ to $-1.98$). RRF-guided item filtering is a prerequisite for IRT convergence ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random). Simulation establishes $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$ at AUC $\geq 0.55$, above current practice ($J = 8$--$30$).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Contamination detection and mitigation.}
Detection methods flag contaminated items via token-level surprisal \citep{shi_2023_minkprob,zhang_2024_minkpp}, accuracy gaps on paraphrases \citep{dekoninck2024constat}, kernel divergence \citep{choi_2025_kds}, completion probes \citep{dong_2024_cdd}, and parallel test sets \citep{zhang_2024_gsm1k}; see \citet{chen2025benchmarking,singh2024evaluation} for surveys. Concurrent work by \citet{tao2025detecting} targets RL-trained models via entropy collapse. These methods perform binary classification without quantifying effect sizes. On mitigation, \citet{sun2025emperor} find none of twenty approaches reliably restores clean performance; DCR \citep{xu2025dcr} estimates decontaminated scores but without uncertainty quantification. ContamCal consumes detection signals as inputs and produces calibrated ability with credible intervals.

\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}
Item Response Theory \citep{lord_1980_irt,hambleton_1991_irt} has gained traction for LLM evaluation: TinyBenchmarks \citep{polo_2024_tinybenchmarks} for efficient evaluation, Growing Pains \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains} for large-scale validation, HiBayES \citep{luettgau_2025_hibayes} for hierarchical Bayesian inference; see \citet{lalor_2024_irt_nlp} for a tutorial. Most directly related, \citet{han2026quantifying} apply psychometric DIF to quantify contamination on item parameters, using group-based binary indicators on naturally contaminated models. ContamCal differs by introducing continuous exposure covariates ($\gamma_k$) from multiple detection signals, operating on controlled-injection data with known ground truth, and providing domain-level decomposition. Our $\gamma$ parameter is formally an instance of explanatory DIF \citep{deboeck_2004_explanatory_irt,eckerly_2017_item_compromise}: the contamination covariate differentially affects response probability, analogous to item compromise models in educational measurement \citep{sinharay_2017_preknowledge}. We use ``calibration'' in the psychometric sense of adjusting scores to approximate uncontaminated performance, distinct from probability calibration \citep{guo_2017_calibration}.

